vintermann wrote:
> If only nethack was implemented with an embedded scripting language, it
> could be trivial to add variety like thousand different door types.

Not necessarily true at all!  It could easily be the case that the
scripting language/engine interface was not set up for this, and then
it becomes quite complex to add this functionality, maybe more
complex because you have to write back-end code that also obeys the
interface.

And it could also easily be the case that while this door stuff
you want to do *is* possible in the scripting language, that there
is a lot of other door stuff being done in the scripting language
as well, and the code is rather opaque, mature, and liable to
spring leaks when poked with a stick. In other words, same crap
different language.

Or maybe even more crap, because the existing scripting code might
have developed some bizarre workarounds and hacks on the interface
functions that would have been a lot less opaque if the programmers
could have done stuff more directly.

> Since nethack already has a lot of variety, the executable is huge,
> and now it seems to me that the dev team rejects perfectly reasonable
> patches out of size concerns.

I'm not sure what concerns these are. It can't be the size of
the distributable, because game logic takes up space whether it's
in the binary, a text file, or a script; and the binary might
be the most space-saving location of these three.

It can't be the conceptual "interaction space" for the game,
since game logic is game logic wherever it lies. NetHack would
have the same level of cruft it it had been in a Lua from day one.
While it might be true that the Dev Team rejects patches because
they would be taxing to implement or maintain because of the
already large game complexity (e.g. "special door idea requires
rethinking the riding code") a scripting language wouldn't help.

It could be the in-memory executable your talking about, so
that NetHack runs on last century's computers and today's
glorified wristwatches, and a scripting language might
help with that, I suppose, although I'm not sure if this
is a factor in the DevTeam rejecting patches.  Anyway the
scripting interpreter will gobble up memory, and the more
stuff you have it doing the more you rely on its memory
management which I will wildly assert is not stellar in
most implementations.

Scripting languages are popular in commercial games for
several reasons. The scripting/enging boundary represents the
need to simultaneously develop the engine and the game logic
yet still keep them separate. It shields the gameplay coding
from a quite nasty and complex game engine (and its validation
and build processes). It allows for mod communities to exist
that have no access to the engine source, and allows a company
to market the engine or retool it for the Next Product,
throwing out the gameplay code. It makes it easier to distribute
gameplay changes, not just in terms of download size but in terms
of the overhead of producing Official Binaries of a shared
codebase. It allows greater speed in changes to gameplay code to meet
deadlines ("what, they never got the MonkeySoldier AI to work?
Ugh, have to remove them from the game by 8am.") And in
commercial games when the scripter wants more scripting
functionality out of the interface/engine (which is often),
it's easy for him to throw a stuffed animal at the person
whose job it is to provide it. None of these apply to NetHack.

Of course scripting languages do have benefits. I'm not saying
that the day NetHack is ported to a better language won't be
a joyful event. But that's not the same as wanting a widely
applied scripting langauge.

Actually NetHack already has something like a scripting language (more
like a description language) for dungeon levels. The language
is quite a pain to use, and while it makes some things easier for
non-coders, when you mess with it you have to have
the mentality of "okay, first I find out what's possible, then
I can figure out what I should imagine doing." This is a common
limitation imposed by scripting languages: you can script any
shade of drab you desire. (For all my whining the use of a
scripting language for the level factories in NetHack is
appropriate).

